[FINAL-TEXT] All evidence is now in. Every test from and to `it_lab3` shows zero packet loss, correct IP assignment, proper gateway reachability, working inter-subnet routing, and no firewall or TC rules. The interface TX counter was 0 only before any probe traffic was generated — after our tests it shows normal two-way counters.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk report describes `it_lab3` as completely isolated from the network, unable to reach even its first-hop gateway. Live inspection of the device finds no such condition. `it_lab3` holds the correct IP address (192.168.1.5/24), the correct default gateway (192.168.1.1, router0), and has no firewall rules, traffic-control policies, or interface errors of any kind. Its eth0 is UP/LOWER_UP with zero RX/TX errors or drops. It successfully pinged its same-LAN peer `it_lab1`, the gateway router0, servers on `lan_servers` (pc1 at 1.0.0.5), and the internet-side hosts (pc2 at 128.168.0.2) — all at 0% packet loss in both directions. Reverse-path probes from `pc1` and `router2` reached `it_lab3` equally cleanly. Switch0, which bridges `link_r0_sw0` and `lan_it`, has both ports UP and in STP-forwarding state. No other IT-LAN host shares the 192.168.1.5 address. The RIP routing table on router0 contains full topology knowledge with correct next-hops. In summary, the network is entirely healthy: the fault described in ticket #4630 does not exist in the live lab. The ticket is a false premise — no corrective action is warranted.
=== END ===